  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nTemps are forecast to remain hot through the extended period, with \nmodels indicating that Thu and Fri will be slightly \"cooler\" days.\n\nWednesday and Thursday...Upper ridge axis remains along the eastern \nborder of CO on Wed, then the high pressure centroid shifts back \nwestward on Thu. A rather strong upper shortwave tracks across the \nGreat Basin on Wed and rides the crest of the ridge, across the \nRockies on Thu. This feature corresponds nicely with the model 1000-\n500 mb RH fields, which indicate a healthy slug of moisture sweeping \nacross the region. This will be the trigger and the fuel for more \nwidespread convection across all of the higher terrain both days, \nwith scattered to even likely activity spilling onto the plains as \nwell. Flash flooding threat for area burn scars increase \nsignificantly for these days, and a Flash Flood Watch may be deemed \nnecessary. Look for highs Wed very similar to today, with 80s to \naround 90F for the high valleys, and mid 90s to low 100s for the \nplains. Increased cloud cover on Thu should help knock a couple \ndegrees off the max temp, with 80s for the high valleys and 90s for \nthe plains. \n\nFriday...The upper shortwave exits to the east Fri morning, then the \nhigh pressure ridge builds back in overhead. Moisture trapped under \nthe high will fuel afternoon and evening convection once again, with \nshowers and thunderstorms likely across the higher terrain, and more \nscattered in nature for the lower elevations. Plan on highs similar \nto readings on Thu.\n\nSaturday through Monday...The heat returns, as high pressure \noverhead remains firmly in place. Moisture trapped under the high \nwill recirculate each day, fueling likely afternoon and evening \nactivity across the mts, and scattered elsewhere. Flash flood \nconcerns will continue, though it could be more spotty in nature. \nMoore",
